\details{
The algorithm has three stages: (1) \code{extract_ancestors()} grows each
variable's ancestor set by repeatedly scanning variable subsets; (2)
\code{extract_parents()} narrows ancestor sets down to direct parents; (3)
\code{extract_vars_sharing_confounders()} tests remaining parent-free pairs for
a shared latent confounder. \code{NA} entries in \code{adjacency_matrix} mean the
corresponding pair is suspected to share a latent confounder, not that no
relationship was estimated.

\code{max_explanatory_num} controls both statistical power and computational
cost: stage 1 scans \code{choose(n_features, k)} subsets for each subset size
\code{k} up to \code{max_explanatory_num + 1}, and each subset requires several
HSIC tests (each O(n^2) in the sample size when \code{independence = "hsic"}).
Cost grows quickly with both the number of variables and \code{n}.

\code{MLHSICR = TRUE} replaces the OLS residual in the independence check with
a residual obtained by directly minimizing the sum of HSIC statistics
between the residual and each explanatory variable via numerical
optimization (\code{stats::optim(method = "L-BFGS-B")}). This can recover
independence in cases where OLS cannot, but requires re-optimizing for
